[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The router0<->router1 physical WAN link is healthy; the outage stems from router1’s routing daemon, not a cable or interface failure. Direct evidence:
- Interface status: router0 eth2 (10.16.1.1/24) and router1 eth2 (10.16.1.2/24) are both UP/LOWER_UP, and ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on both ends.
- Data-plane test: ICMP between the peers succeeds bidirectionally (router0 → 10.16.1.2 OK; router1 → 10.16.1.1 OK).

However, router1’s control plane is down:
- FRR service on router1 is inactive (dead) and its running config is effectively empty (no RIP/OSPF/BGP), so router1 is not learning/advertising routes. Router0 still has routes via other neighbors, so some inter-zone paths work, but any traffic that requires router1 as a transit loses reachability—matching the reported “partial” impact while hosts still reach their local gateways.

Localization:
- Physical link: wan_link_r0_r1 on router0 eth2 and router1 eth2 (both operational).
- Fault: router1 FRRouting (daemon/config), not an interface.

Root cause: A change on router1 stopped/removed its FRR routing process and configuration, halting route exchange and causing partial inter-zone connectivity loss despite the WAN link being up.

Corrective fix:
- Restore FRR on router1: reapply the intended routing configuration (e.g., RIP/OSPF/BGP as designed) and start/enable the frr service. Verify adjacencies to router0 and router2 and confirm full route tables are populated. No cable/interface replacement is required on the router0<->router1 link. === END ===